{
  "title": "Triplane Fractures",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/pediatrics/4029/triplane-fractures",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Mar 7 2026",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Triplane Fractures are traumatic ankle fractures seen in children 10-17 years of age characterized by a complex salter harris IV fracture pattern in multiple planes.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis can be made with plain radiographs of the ankle. CT scan may be required to further characterize the fracture pattern and for surgical planning.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is closed reduction and casting or surgical fixation depending on the patient age and degree of fracture displacement.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Incidence",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "accounts for 5-15% of pediatric ankle fractures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Demographics",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "more common in males",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "occurs in children during physeal closure (average age is 13 years old)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "juvenile ankle physis ossifies in specific order, which leads to **transitional fractures** such as triplane and tillaux fractures",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**younger** than tillaux fracture age group",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Pathophysiology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "mechanism of injury",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "lateral triplane fractures",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "results from **supination-external rotation injury**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "similar to tillaux fractures",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "1",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "medial triplane fractures",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "results from **adduction injury**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "pathoanatomy",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "a complex **SH IV fracture** pattern with components in all three planes",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "may be 2, 3, or 4 part fractures",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "sagittal plane - epiphysis is often fractured on the lateral aspect in the **sagittal plane** (same as tillaux fracture) and is seen on the AP radiograph",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "5",
                          "children": []
                        },
                        {
                          "text": "axial plane - physis is separated",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "2",
                          "children": []
                        },
                        {
                          "text": "coronal plane - metaphysis is fractured on the posterior aspect in the **coronal plane and is seen on the lateral radiograph**",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "5",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Associated conditions",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "fibular fractures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "occurs in 50%",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "typically spiral fracture located proximal to the physis in children nearing skeletal maturity",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "remains unstable after fixation of tibia, so fixation of fibula is usually necessary",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "ipsilateral tibial shaft fractures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Physeal considerations",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "distal tibial physis",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "accounts for 35-40% of overall tibial growth and 15-20% of overall lower extremity growth",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "rate of growth is 3-4 mm/year",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "growth continues until 14 years in girls and 16 years in boys",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "closure occurs during an 18 month transitional period",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "**occurs in a predictable pattern: central > anteromedial > posteromedial > lateral**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Classification",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Parts",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "",
          "table": [
            {
              "header": true,
              "cells": [
                {
                  "colspan": 3,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "**Classification by Parts**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "header": false,
              "cells": [
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "2-part",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "Part 1 - anterolateral and posterior epiphysis is connected to the posterior metaphyseal fragment",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Part 2 - anteromedial epiphysis is connected to the remainder of the distal tibia",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [
                        {
                          "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4029/images/2-part.jpg",
                          "caption": "2 part"
                        }
                      ],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "header": false,
              "cells": [
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "3-part",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "Part 1 - anteriolateral epiphysis",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Part 2 - posterior epiphysis is connected to the posterior metaphyseal fragment",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Part 3 - anteromedial epiphysis is connected to the remainder of the distal tibia",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [
                        {
                          "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4029/images/3part.jpg",
                          "caption": "3 part"
                        }
                      ],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "header": false,
              "cells": [
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "4-part",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "Comminuted variant",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Can only be distinguished from 3-part fractures via CT",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [
                        {
                          "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4029/images/4-part.jpg",
                          "caption": "4 part"
                        }
                      ],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Pattern",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "",
          "table": [
            {
              "header": true,
              "cells": [
                {
                  "colspan": 3,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "**Classification by Pattern**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "header": false,
              "cells": [
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "Lateral triplane fracture",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "**Most common**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Epiphyseal fracture occurs in the **sagittal plane**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Physeal fracture occurs in the axial plane",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Metaphyseal fracture occurs in the **coronal plane**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Similar to tillaux fractures on AP radiographs (distinguish from tillaux fractures by SH II or I fracture on lateral radiograph)",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [
                        {
                          "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4029/images/lateral.jpg",
                          "caption": "Lateral"
                        }
                      ],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "header": false,
              "cells": [
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "Medial triplane fracture",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "Epiphyseal fracture occurs in the **coronal plane**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Physeal fracture occurs in the axial plane",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Metaphyseal fracture occurs in the **sagittal plane**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [
                        {
                          "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4029/images/medial.jpg",
                          "caption": "Medial"
                        }
                      ],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "header": false,
              "cells": [
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "Intramalleolar triplane fracture",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "Type I - intraarticular intramalleolar fracture involving the weight-bearing surface",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Type II - intraarticular intramalleolar fracture outside of the weight-bearing surface",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Type III - extraarticular intramalleolar fracture",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "colspan": 0,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [
                        {
                          "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4029/images/intramall.jpg",
                          "caption": "Intramalleolar"
                        }
                      ],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
